g', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2019-10-01T04:10:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'NYTNow', 'section_name': 'Briefing', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melina Delkic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Delkic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/dedddf93-ae91-50f4-8e8e-c3cc72d00f3e', 'word_count': 1419, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dedddf93-ae91-50f4-8e8e-c3cc72d00f3e'}, {'abstract': 'Several fishing boat workers were among at least a dozen people injured when the bridge, built in 1998, fell into a waterway in a fishing village.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/01/world/asia/taiwan-bridge-collapse.html', 'snippet': 'Several fishing boat workers were among at least a dozen people injured when the bridge, built in 1998, fell into a waterway in a fishing village.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A bridge collapsed in northeast Taiwan on Tuesday morning while an oil tanker truck was crossing it, sending the truck crashing onto fishing boats and launching rescuers on a desperate search for missing victims, according to local officials and news reports.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bridge Collapses in Taiwan; Oil Truck Plunges Moments Away From Crossing', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Taiwan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-10-01T04:42:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Express', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Daniel Victor and Chris Horton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Daniel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Victor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/636073d9-344c-5c6d-a375-06a8c50323c6', 'word_count': 357, 'uri': 'nyt://article/636073d9-344c-5c6d-a375-06a8c50323c6'}, {'abstract': 'Tolerance of diversity, not repression, is the surest way to govern a vast territory with many peoples.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/01/opinion/xi-jinping-china.html', 'snippet': 'Tolerance of diversity, not repression, is the surest way to govern a vast territory with many peoples.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the occasion of the 70th anniversary of the People’s Republic of China (P.R.C.) on Oct. 1, the party-state has much to celebrate: an unprecedented record of economic development, world-class education and technological innovation, an increasingly prominent position on the world stage. But even as the authorities go to extreme lengths to assure a triumphant birthday parade, the Chinese Communist Party (C.C.P.) faces its most intense international criticism since 1989, when it killed hundreds of unarmed protesters in the heart of Beijing, at Tiananmen Square. Thirty years later, international concern is focused on China’s peripheries: Xinjiang and Hong Kong.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/09/30/opinion/30millward1/30millward1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/09/30/opinion/30millward1/30millward1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/09/30/opinion/30millward1/30millward1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 