': 'glocations', 'value': 'Beijing (China)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hong Kong', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-10-01T17:23:28+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noor Gill', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noor', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gill', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/4fc9aebd-1550-517a-b73e-5de1d0f629b5', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/4fc9aebd-1550-517a-b73e-5de1d0f629b5'}, {'abstract': 'A Greek-inspired restaurant from a Noma alumnus, a brief pop-up for a meat-and-beer veteran, and more restaurant news.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/01/dining/nyc-restaurant-openings.html', 'snippet': 'A Greek-inspired restaurant from a Noma alumnus, a brief pop-up for a meat-and-beer veteran, and more restaurant news.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The chefs Taka Sakaeda and Jihan Lee, who both worked at the sushi master Masa Takayama’s restaurants, are behind separate counters in this new restaurant’s elegant pale-wood dining room. (The operations director, Lisa Limb, also worked at Mr. Takayama’s restaurants.) But unlike at Masa, the specialties here are temaki hand rolls. And the temaki are not cylindrical or cone-shaped. They’re open, with a crisp sheet of nori seaweed wrapping the filling like a taco. “They’re not traditional, but we’re also doing simpler classics for the purists,” Mr. Sakaeda said. Among the signature combinations are sea scallops in XO sauce with tobiko and lemon, salmon and tomato with onion cream, and tuna poke-style with crisp shallots. The classics include spicy tuna, and yellowtail with scallions. There are also some vegan selections. Starters include nori chips with a yogurt dip, and a cucumber tataki with wasabi. Temaki desserts incorporate ice cream and fruit. There’s a sleek bar, a few tables up front, and a semi-secluded table for six in the rear. The whole space seats 40. (Opens Wednesday) ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/02/dining/01off/merlin_161630526_fb672091-0677-4f86-b0b1-0046b50cb3fb-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/02/dining/01off/merlin_161630526_fb672091-0677-4f86-b0b1-0046b50cb3fb-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/02/dining/01off/merlin_161630526_fb672091-0677-4f86-b0b1-0046b50cb3fb-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/02/dining/01off/merlin_161630526_fb672091-0677-4f86-b0b1-0046b50cb3fb-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/02/dining/01off/01off-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/10/02/dining/01off/01off-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/02/dining/01off/01off-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Temaki Hand Rolls With a Twist in the West Village', 'kicker': 'off the menu', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 