 owner’s “only friend,” and became front-page news when he was discovered in 2003.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Antoine Yates’s neighbors took notice of the exotic little pet he brought back one day to his Harlem housing project 20 years ago.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/03/nyregion/03TIGER-print/00TIGER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/03/nyregion/03TIGER-print/00TIGER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/03/nyregion/03TIGER-print/00TIGER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/03/nyregion/03TIGER-print/00TIGER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/03/nyregion/03TIGER-print/00TIGER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/10/03/nyregion/03TIGER-print/00TIGER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/03/nyregion/03TIGER-print/00TIGER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Ming, the Bengal Tiger Raised in a Harlem Apartment, Has Died', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'R.I.P. Ming, Bengal Tiger of Harlem', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tigers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Harlem (Manhattan, NY)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Animal Abuse, Rights and Welfare', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-10-02T15:35:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Corey Kilgannon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Corey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kilgannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/da14c42f-d34a-572d-a96e-974663c24a8c', 'word_count': 879, 'uri': 'nyt://article/da14c42f-d34a-572d-a96e-974663c24a8c'}, {'abstract': 'Critics seem to believe in the Joker’s evil powers even more than the most die-hard comic books fans do.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/02/magazine/the-joker-movie.html', 'snippet': 'Critics seem to believe in the Joker’s evil powers even more than the most die-hard comic books fans do.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a slightly different culture, an art film about the Joker would be a premise for a comedy sketch. The beats write themselves: You start with shots of urban alienation, maybe some melancholy piano as a damaged man tries and fails to make ’em laugh. You work up that somber atmosphere for a minute or so, then push things over the line with a shot of him putting on the makeup and doing some crimes. If you really wanted to work the bathos, you could end with a couple bars of “Send in the Clowns.” Taking a dumb idea very seriously is an evergreen comic conceit; in 1999 it might have been a sketch on MadTV.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/06/magazine/06Scr