ard'}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/fee7ade7-d275-5137-95c1-37c291e25084', 'word_count': 677, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fee7ade7-d275-5137-95c1-37c291e25084'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Klee’s late paintings; Anni Albers’s rhythmic textiles; Sean Donnola’s films and photographs; Marcus Jahmal’s latest works; Stephen Prina’s lithographs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/02/arts/design/new-york-galleries-what-to-see-right-now.html', 'snippet': 'Paul Klee’s late paintings; Anni Albers’s rhythmic textiles; Sean Donnola’s films and photographs; Marcus Jahmal’s latest works; Stephen Prina’s lithographs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 1937, Paul Klee’s paintings were included in what might have been the best-attended art show of the 20th century: the “Degenerate Art” exhibition in Munich. The Bauhaus, his old school, was long shut; “Angelus Novus,” his early masterpiece of a gasping seraph, languished in the luggage of the exiled philosopher Walter Benjamin; and Klee, who’d left Germany for his native Switzerland, turned his ironic eye to a continent losing its mind for the second time.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/04/arts/03galleries-klee2/03galleries-klee2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 425, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/04/arts/03galleries-klee2/03galleries-klee2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 425}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/04/arts/03galleries-klee2/03galleries-klee2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 725, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/04/arts/03galleries-klee2/03galleries-klee