een challenging our notions of identity and selfhood for three decades, but her latest project feels uniquely pressing.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/02/t-magazine/catherine-opie.html', 'snippet': 'The artist has been challenging our notions of identity and selfhood for three decades, but her latest project feels uniquely pressing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After the 2016 presidential election, the artist Catherine Opie began cutting up magazines and collecting images of political import: photographs of guns, senators, baby seals on shrinking ice caps. Today, in her studio in downtown Los Angeles, which occupies 5,000 square feet of a former brewery, a free-standing glossy black monitor plays these images as part of an animated collage. As digitized cutouts of ocean scenes and architectural landmarks inch upward on the screen, Opie explains, they illustrate a dire consequence of our rising seas: “Buildings are going to go underwater.”', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/02/t-magazine/02tmag-opie-slide-KQ6Z/02tmag-opie-slide-KQ6Z-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 800, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/02/t-magazine/02tmag-opie-slide-KQ6Z/02tmag-opie-slide-KQ6Z-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 800}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/02/t-magazine/02tmag-opie-slide-KQ6Z/02tmag-opie-slide-KQ6Z-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/02/t-magazine/02tmag-opie-slide-KQ6Z/02tmag-opie-slide-KQ6Z-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1536, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'super