', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5b8bc0b3-24b4-5616-bac0-9deffd143528', 'word_count': 1706, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5b8bc0b3-24b4-5616-bac0-9deffd143528'}, {'abstract': 'Succinct phrases and sentences pack a deep power of knowledge and conviction. One thing — but also everything else. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/03/magazine/poem-crossing.html', 'snippet': 'Succinct phrases and sentences pack a deep power of knowledge and conviction. One thing — but also everything else. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'A poem may be deceptively small in size but no less mesmerizing. Read “Crossing” aloud a few times, and the mouth and lungs fall into a different rhythm and pacing. “The Tradition,” Jericho Brown’s stunning book, contains so many moments of utter hypnosis like this — a long stare, a conversation constructed of mesmerizing layers, many unspoken, but moving far, far beyond a moment. Succinct phrases and sentences pack a deep power of knowledge and conviction. One thing — but also everything else. Let’s dedicate this poem to the Bahamas and all the energy it’s going to take to put that beautiful world back together. Let’s dedicate it to every next day. Selected by Naomi Shihab Nye', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/06/magazine/06Poem/06Poem-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 215, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/06/magazine/06Poem/06Poem-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 215}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/06/magazine/06Poem/06Poem-jumbo-v3.jpg', 'height': 367, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 