timedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Associated Press', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Associated Press'}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/818a8ed6-7127-5712-ad0b-739e3d36a499', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/818a8ed6-7127-5712-ad0b-739e3d36a499'}, {'abstract': 'Governments are spending a remarkable amount of resources attacking books — because their supposed limitations are beginning to look like ageless strengths.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/03/opinion/books-censorship.html', 'snippet': 'Governments are spending a remarkable amount of resources attacking books — because their supposed limitations are beginning to look like ageless strengths.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Around the world, many authoritarian regimes — having largely corralled the internet — now have declared war on the written word, their oldest enemy. The received wisdom after the close of the Cold War was that physical books were outdated, soon to be swept aside in the digital age; and that the internet was instead the real threat to governments seeking to repress provocative thinking. A generation later, the opposite may be true.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/01/opinion/01white/01white-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 720, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/01/opinion/01white/01white-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 720}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/01/opinion/01white/01white-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 854, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/01/opinion/01white/01white-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1707, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/01/opinion/01white/01white-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/10/01/opinion/01white/01white-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/01/opinion/01white/01white-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Authoritarian’s Worst Fear? A Book', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Censorship', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Causeway Bay Books (Causeway Bay, Hong Kong, Retailer)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-10-03T15:48:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Duncan White', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Duncan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'White', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/cbe4a8af-9a3c-5583-9810-630a806f44a1', 'word_count': 1068, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cbe4a8af-9a3c-5583-9810-630a806f44a1'}, {'abstract': 'The artist’s photographs, a major act of urban historical preservation, are on view at the Bronx Museum of the Arts.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/03/arts/design/henry-chalfant-graffiti-bronx.html', 'snippet': 'The artist’s photographs, a major act of urban historical preservation, are on view at the Bronx Museum of the Arts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Henry Chalfant arrived in New York City from suburban Pittsburgh in 1973, as an aspiring sculptor, he found a place teetering on the verge of bankruptcy. This was “Ford to City: Drop Dead” New York. But amid the turmoil a new form of art making was taking shape — one that took up space where it could, which was mostly everywhere.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/04/arts/04chalfant2/merlin_161864175_cea18e9c-9711-44f8-8ebe-e6bd3307eb16-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/04/arts/04chalfant2/merlin_161864175_cea18e9c-9711-44f8-8ebe-e6bd3307eb16-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/04/arts/04chalfant2/merlin_161864175_cea18e9c-9711-44f8-8ebe-e6bd3307eb16-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/04/arts/04chalfant2/merlin_161864175_cea18e9c-9711-44f8-8ebe-e6bd3307eb16-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/04/arts/04chalfant2/merlin_161864175_cea18e9c-9711-44f8-8ebe-e6bd3307eb16-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/10/04/arts/04chalfant2/merlin_161864175_cea18e9c-9711-44f8