', 'value': 'Translation and Interpreters', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Opera', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gershwin, George', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gershwin, Ira', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Porgy and Bess (Opera)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-10-03T16:29:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Grode', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Grode', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4ad4a77-8913-5b96-aa67-dcb07a489976', 'word_count': 878, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4ad4a77-8913-5b96-aa67-dcb07a489976'}, {'abstract': 'Based on a novella by Stephen King and Joe Hill, this Netflix adaptation turns rural Kansas into an inescapable nightmare.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/03/movies/in-the-tall-grass-review.html', 'snippet': 'Based on a novella by Stephen King and Joe Hill, this Netflix adaptation turns rural Kansas into an inescapable nightmare.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Originally published in two parts in Esquire, the novella “In the Tall Grass,” by Stephen King and his son Joe Hill, turns the uniform expanse of rural Kansas into a field that stretches to the horizon in every direction. The field itself has a malevolent presence, like the sentient vines in Scott Smith’s novel “The Ruins,” but just the idea of getting trapped in a sea of green, with the sticky heat and the buzzing flies and the six-foot-tall cuts of grass, is suffocating enough for a short-form shocker.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/03/arts/03tallgrass/merlin_