colman/04tmag-colman-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Read T a Poem | Colman Domingo', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Domingo, Colman', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-10-04T16:08:40+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Barbara Anastacio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Barbara', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Anastacio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/d8699294-b4d8-530e-90b3-09cffb6f65a5', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/d8699294-b4d8-530e-90b3-09cffb6f65a5'}, {'abstract': 'The multitalented star of the new movie “Lucy in the Sky” recites a work by Ed Bok Lee.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/04/t-magazine/colman-domingo-poem.html', 'snippet': 'The multitalented star of the new movie “Lucy in the Sky” recites a work by Ed Bok Lee.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The actor, writer and director Colman Domingo, 49, has always loved poetry. “I grew up on poems by E.E. Cummings, Langston Hughes, Maya Angelou, Nikki Giovanni and Paul Laurence Dunbar,” he says. In addition to starring alongside Natalie Portman in the new film “Lucy in the Sky,” which follows an astronaut’s struggle to readjust to life on Earth after traveling into space, Domingo recently appeared on HBO’s “Euphoria” and is a co-writer of the acclaimed “Summer: Donna Summer the Musical,” which is currently on tour across the U.S. When he’s crafting narratives for any of his projects, whether he’s in front of the camera or behind the scenes, he often returns to the words of his favorite writers. “Poems influenced my love of craftsmanship in telling a story,” he says, “whether in short prose or long form.” The works he revisits again and again have in common “rich language, impeccable phrasing, soul stirring images, and wit,” he says, and so it makes sense that for T’s video series “Read T a Poem,” he chose to recite a work by the South Korean-born American writer Ed Bok Lee, whose insightful, exacting poems reflect the intimate ways globalization is transforming our culture and our lives. Domingo’s selection, “Summer Open Window,” is from Lee’s latest collection, “Mitochondrial Night,” which uses DNA as a central theme in its lyrical exploration of genealogy, geography and time.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/04/t-magazine/04tmag-colman-02/04tmag-colman-02-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/04/t-magazine/04tmag-colman-02/04tmag-colman-02-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/04/t-magazine/04tmag-colman-02/04tmag-colman-02-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/04/t-magazine/04tmag-colman-02/04tmag-colman-02-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/04/t-magazine/04tmag-colman-02/04tmag-colman-02-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', '