ious plan of President Recep Tayyip Erdogan of Turkey to resettle at least one million Syrian refugees within a “safe zone” along the Turkish-Syrian border has come a step closer to being realized. On Sunday President Trump endorsed a Turkish military operation to carve out the “safe zone” in northeastern Syria, currently controlled by the United States-supported, Kurdish-led Syrian Democratic Forces.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/07/opinion/07gingerasdanforth1/merlin_162249081_a5397a89-596a-4fb7-8b15-fadd5a375569-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/07/opinion/07gingerasdanforth1/merlin_162249081_a5397a89-596a-4fb7-8b15-fadd5a375569-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/07/opinion/07gingerasdanforth1/merlin_162249081_a5397a89-596a-4fb7-8b15-fadd5a375569-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/07/opinion/07gingerasdanforth1/merlin_162249081_a5397a89-596a-4fb7-8b15-fadd5a375569-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/07/opinion/07gingerasdanforth1/07gingerasdanforth1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/10/07/opinion/07gingerasdanforth1/07gingerasdanforth1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/07/opinion/07gingerasdanforth1/07gingerasdanforth1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Turkey’s Plan to Move Refugees to Syria Is Dangerous', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kurds', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ottoman Empire', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Erdogan, Recep Tayyip', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ANKARA (TURKEY)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-10-07T15:34:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ryan Gingeras and Nick Danforth', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ryan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gingeras', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Danforth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/04343680-ee43-5f8c-aab9-7d187d649924', 'word_count': 967, 'uri': 'nyt://article/04343680-ee43-5f8c-aab9-7d187d649924'}, {'abstract': 'In Steve Sheinkin’s thrilling “Born to Fly,” the mechanical hurdles seem hard enough. Then comes the nonstop scolding.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/07/books/review/born-to-fly-steve-sheinken.html', 'snippet': 'In Steve Sheinkin’s thrilling “Born to Fly,” the mechanical hurdles seem hard enough. Then comes the nonstop scolding.', 'lead_paragraph': '“They were the kinds of kids who jumped off the roofs of buildings,” begins Steve Sheinkin’s stirring account of the 1929 Air Derby, BORN TO FLY: The First Women’s Air Race Across America (Roaring Brook, 288 pp., $19.99; ages 8 to 12), without a hint of hyperbole. Future participants in the first women’s cross-country air race did indeed spend their childhoods leaping from outlandish heights with umbrella parachutes, greasing the tracks of rickety homemade roller coasters and building honest-to-goodness airplanes in the backyard. Asked why she’d taken to the skies, Florence “Pancho” Barnes once retorted, “To keep from exploding — that’s why I fly!”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/09/29/books/review/29Miller-KIDS/29Miller-KIDS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 300, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/09/29/books/review/29Miller-KIDS/29Miller-KIDS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 300}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/09/29/books/review/29Miller-KIDS/29Miller-KIDS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 512, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/09/29/books/review/29Miller-KIDS/29Miller-KIDS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1000, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/09/29/books/review/29Miller-KIDS/29Miller-KIDS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/09/29/books/review/29Miller-KIDS/29Miller-KIDS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/09/29/books/review/29Miller-KIDS/29Miller-KIDS-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Women Fliers Who Kept Their Heads in the Clouds', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '20th-Century History', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sheinkin, Steve', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': "Born to Fly: The First Women's Air Race Across America (Book)", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-10-07T15:52:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4fe1df37-510e-5317-913e-c8c64ab03873', 'word_count': 673, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4fe1df37-510e-5317-913e-c8c64ab03873'}, {'abstract': 'Don Brown’s graphic nonfiction book “Fever Year” skillfully brings young readers directly into the (gruesome) action.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/07/books/review/fever-year-don-brown.html', 'snippet': 'Don Brown’s graphic nonfiction book “Fever Year” skillfully brings young readers directly into the (gruesome) action.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Middle school students often tell me that history is boring. “We just memorize facts about dead people,” they say. Already, these 12- and 13-year-olds have come to see historical knowledge as little more than a list of names and dates. All breadth and no depth, the history they’ve encountered has been stripped of meaning. Where are the specific people? Where are the personal stories? No wonder so many kids are indifferent to our past.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/09/29/books/review/29Fleming-SUB01/29Fleming-SUB01-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 917, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/09/29/books/review/29Fleming-SUB01/29Fleming-SUB01-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 917}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/09/29/books/review/29Fleming-SUB01/29Fleming-SUB01-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 670, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/09/29/books/review/29Fleming-SUB01/29Fleming-SUB01-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1340, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/09/29/books/review/29Fleming-SUB01/29Fleming-SUB01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/09/29/books/review/29Fleming-SUB01/29Fleming-SUB01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/09/29/books/review/29Fleming-SUB01/29Fleming-SUB01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Deadly 1918 Flu Epidemic Gets a Fresh Treatment', 'kicker': 'Children’s Books', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None