rticle/27b58899-1e82-52c7-b7f9-261249b5e75e'}, {'abstract': 'New research shows that hundreds of North American birds are at  risk of major habitat disruption from climate change.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/10/climate/state-birds-climate-change.html', 'snippet': 'New research shows that hundreds of North American birds are at  risk of major habitat disruption from climate change.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Each state in America has an official state bird, usually an iconic species that helps define the landscape. Minnesota chose the common loon, whose haunting wails echo across the state’s northern lakes each summer. Georgia picked the brown thrasher, a fiercely territorial bird with a repertoire of more than 1,000 song types.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/11/climate/00cli-birds-07/00cli-birds-07-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/11/climate/00cli-birds-07/00cli-birds-07-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/11/climate/00cli-birds-07/00cli-birds-07-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/11/climate/00cli-birds-07/00cli-birds-07-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1333, 'width': 2000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/11/climate/00cli-birds-07/00cli-birds-07-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': '