': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Sanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5512fb88-87c8-5c76-9fe6-f7087204a34b', 'word_count': 630, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5512fb88-87c8-5c76-9fe6-f7087204a34b'}, {'abstract': 'In new graphic novels (one by Kristen Gudsnuk, the other by Shannon Hale and LeUyen Pham), the key to middle school relationships is being true to yourself. If that fails, try magic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/11/books/review/best-friends-shannon-hale-leuyen-pham.html', 'snippet': 'In new graphic novels (one by Kristen Gudsnuk, the other by Shannon Hale and LeUyen Pham), the key to middle school relationships is being true to yourself. If that fails, try magic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Normal girls in their tween years are self-conscious. They worry in equal measure about being seen and not being seen. They worry about their clothes and whether they’re listening to the right music. But cool girls, ah, the cool girls. They seem to exist without being concerned about what anybody else thinks. They seem to fit in without trying and, most irritatingly, without caring.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/10/books/review/10Kolben-2/10Kolben-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 868, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/10/books/review/10Kolben-2/10Kolben-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 868}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/10/books/review/10Kolben-2/10Kolben-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 708, 'subType': 'jumbo