 Generation Cartel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Michoacan (Mexico)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-10-14T20:39:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabeth Malkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Malkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9cc2cc31-5583-5b57-9f0f-fe8dcd6dfddf', 'word_count': 606, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9cc2cc31-5583-5b57-9f0f-fe8dcd6dfddf'}, {'abstract': 'In “Rusty Brown,” Chris Ware spans lives, generations and even universes. But somehow all roads lead back to Nebraska, where he grew up.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/14/books/review/rusty-brown-chris-ware.html', 'snippet': 'In “Rusty Brown,” Chris Ware spans lives, generations and even universes. But somehow all roads lead back to Nebraska, where he grew up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For the graphic novelist Chris Ware, God is in the details. In 2004, he edited a special comics issue of McSweeney’s Quarterly, and as with his own titles, even the unfoldable dust jacket teemed with extra texts and gags. Here, on what normally functions as decoration, Ware concealed a story in which God wonders what happened to “that planet where I made everyone in my own image. Drolly rendered as a stack of colored circles with stringlike limbs, the supreme creator goes on to muse that Earth had some good things — including comic strips — which leads him to pity the sad lives of the slobs who drew them: “I wonder why other people couldn’t see the virtues of an innately democratic pictographic poetry, grounded in a transdimensional metaphysic, anyway?”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None,