ier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a7deeb34-f835-5cd2-8e6f-c8fb4032fffe', 'word_count': 112, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a7deeb34-f835-5cd2-8e6f-c8fb4032fffe'}, {'abstract': 'Stony Brook WholeHeartedFoods and Autumn Seed sell their pumpkin seeds to a variety of specialty food purveyors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/14/dining/pumpkin-seeds-america.html', 'snippet': 'Stony Brook WholeHeartedFoods and Autumn Seed sell their pumpkin seeds to a variety of specialty food purveyors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Those pumpkin seeds you might buy to nibble, use in cooking or mix into granola probably come from China, the world’s largest producer of pumpkins. Two American producers of pumpkin seeds — Stony Brook WholeHeartedFoods in Geneva, N.Y., and Autumn Seed in Corvallis, Ore. — estimate that at least 75 percent of the seeds sold in the United States are imported from China, where cheap labor for hulling the seeds keeps the price well below what American producers charge.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/16/dining/14burner2/merlin_162005061_e26acd31-48a4-45db-a297-93af4c99b36b-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 482, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/16/dining/14burner2/merlin_162005061_e26acd31-48a4-45db-a297-93af4c99b36b-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 482}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/16/dining/14burner2/merlin_162005061_e26acd31-48a4-45db-a297-93af4c99b36b-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 822, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': '