rticle', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emily Rapp Black', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emily', 'middlename': 'Rapp', 'lastname': 'Black', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4b93e372-a49e-5b29-abe7-9698d6929fd3', 'word_count': 1155, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4b93e372-a49e-5b29-abe7-9698d6929fd3'}, {'abstract': 'I don’t know who needs to hear this but stop scrolling on Instagram at night and start meditating.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/15/style/self-care/bedtime-routine-benefits.html', 'snippet': 'I don’t know who needs to hear this but stop scrolling on Instagram at night and start meditating.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When my better angels are in charge of my schedule — instead of the insatiable gremlin that won’t get off Instagram — I end the day by starting my bedtime routine: lighting candles; eating early, (three-ish hours before going to sleep, in a knockoff version of intermittent fasting, it makes for better digestion and for me, fewer nightmares); molting daytime clothes and obligations (no screens, so no social media, no texting, no email), and then floating around for 20 minutes of Vedic meditation; some at-home hypnotherapy; a little journaling; reading a book that asks nothing of me; and listing five “happinesses,” just some small things that I want to keep close. ', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/15/style/self-care/BEDTIMEROUTINE2/oakImage-1571147796692-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/15/style/self-care/BEDTIMEROUTINE2/oakImage-1571147796692-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600,