rganization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1c6dcf09-0c17-52c6-8d69-0d9d2fdb4eb7', 'word_count': 1470, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1c6dcf09-0c17-52c6-8d69-0d9d2fdb4eb7'}, {'abstract': 'Franz Erhard Walther’s canvas sculptures, Jesse Mockrin’s paintings from another century; Greg Parma Smith’s crescent moons and unicorns.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/16/arts/design/new-york-galleries-what-to-see-right-now.html', 'snippet': 'Franz Erhard Walther’s canvas sculptures, Jesse Mockrin’s paintings from another century; Greg Parma Smith’s crescent moons and unicorns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You appreciate the importance of minimalism, but can’t abide its froideur; you appreciate the importance of performance art, but find it indulgent or inscrutable; you appreciate the importance of fiber art, but wonder if it’s really just craft. We can help make sense of this! All you need is Franz Erhard Walther of Germany, whose drawings, gouaches and soft sculptures in his ebullient, lavishly beautiful retrospective, “Migration of Forms, 1956-2006,” at Freeman reveal the power of all these avant-garde tendencies at once.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/18/arts/17galleries-walther1/17galleries-walther1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/18/arts/17galleries-walther1/17galleries-walther1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/18/arts/17galleries-walther1/17galleries-walther1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': No