': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Christoph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Koettl', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Shane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Neill", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}, {'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Horn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 4}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/e27670b0-03d4-52bb-9f09-7ca2c7d5b764', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/e27670b0-03d4-52bb-9f09-7ca2c7d5b764'}, {'abstract': 'We sent the artist an instant camera, and he shared a characteristically joyful glimpse into his world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/16/t-magazine/nick-cave-photos.html', 'snippet': 'We sent the artist an instant camera, and he shared a characteristically joyful glimpse into his world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Chicago-based multidisciplinary artist Nick Cave, one of the four cover subjects of T’s Oct. 20 Greats issue, builds jubilant sanctuaries from seemingly ordinary objects: synthetic hair, buttons and antique-store finds such as chandeliers, lawn jockeys and ceramic fruit. He assembles these materials into joyous embellished costumes, which he calls Soundsuits, and sparkling room-filling installations, which he envisions as ornate protective barriers from America’s political turmoil and racial violence. As Megan O’Grady writes in her profile of the artist, “Cave operates against entrenched hierarchies, elevating glittery consumer detritus and traditional handicrafts like beadwork or sewing to enchanting heights.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/16/t-magazine/art/14tmag-nick-slide-8DBL/14tmag-nick-slide-8DBL-articleLarge