count': 1342, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d46d7343-7a47-5cd2-abef-5d1e56705e8d'}, {'abstract': 'David Romtvedt’s poems  often contain a tangible moment or two, then something startling or surprising drops in. ', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/17/magazine/poem-taking-leave.html', 'snippet': 'David Romtvedt’s poems  often contain a tangible moment or two, then something startling or surprising drops in. ', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of poetry’s most appealing elements can be its mixture of observations. How do these things go together? someone might ask. David Romtvedt’s poems in “Dilemmas of the Angels” often contain a tangible moment or two, then something startling or surprising drops in. These poems of layering counterpoint feel a lot like the world — a person standing on a street corner watching others move by, each in his or her own universe, and then there are the plants, and the ants, and the birds, and the machines, and objects that give the people gravity — and the intentions — where will the people pause next? Why did the birds fly too soon?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/20/magazine/20mag-poem-image1/20mag-poem-image1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 263, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/20/magazine/20mag-poem-image1/20mag-poem-image1-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 263}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/20/magazine/20mag-poem-image1/20mag-poem-image1-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 449, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/20/magazine/20mag-poem-image1/20mag-poem-image1-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 629, 'width': 1434, 'subType': 's