ame': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Religion', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Edmund G Jr', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-10-17T19:15:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Letters', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Letters', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/fa8a8cf9-5904-5b01-a7ff-4bfb2a058e92', 'word_count': 628, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fa8a8cf9-5904-5b01-a7ff-4bfb2a058e92'}, {'abstract': 'Despite widespread corruption in the union’s top ranks, the workers made the fight their own.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/17/opinion/GM-strike-agreement.html', 'snippet': 'Despite widespread corruption in the union’s top ranks, the workers made the fight their own.', 'lead_paragraph': 'United Automobile Workers leaders voted Thursday on a tentative agreement to end a monthlong walkout by 50,000 workers at General Motors over wages, health insurance premiums and the use of temporary and “tiered” contracts. The accord includes raises and a large ratification bonus and gives temps a path to permanent status — but it also locks in the closing of a beloved factory in Lordstown, Ohio, and two others. Rank-and-file members across the country will now vote on whether to accept the deal and return to work.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/18/opinion/17Kim/merlin_160918938_b4a008a4-e09f-4cd4-97ef-65f719a6899d-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/18/opinion/17Kim/merlin_160918938_b4a008a4-e09f-4cd4-97ef-65f719a6899d-a