 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McCabe', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/baf637ca-b073-564d-87a8-14c847035a8a', 'word_count': 605, 'uri': 'nyt://article/baf637ca-b073-564d-87a8-14c847035a8a'}, {'abstract': 'Read these spine-tingling works of science fiction and fantasy at your own risk.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/18/books/review/otherworldly-science-fiction-fantasy-amal-el-mohtar.html', 'snippet': 'Read these spine-tingling works of science fiction and fantasy at your own risk.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The summer’s drawn back its light and now shadows lengthen down our days. It’s finally time to revel in the jeweled darkness of autumn. HIS HIDEOUS HEART (Flatiron, $18.99) is a superb collection of young adult short stories inspired by Edgar Allan Poe’s work. Dahlia Adler gathers 13 (of course) authors — herself included — to retell or reinvent several of Poe’s best-known stories (“The Pit and the Pendulum,” “The Tell-Tale Heart,” “The Purloined Letter”) alongside some of his less anthologized ones (“Hop-Frog,” “The Oval Portrait”), as well as a couple of his famous poems (“The Raven,” “Annabel Lee”). The volume is divided between the new stories and Poe’s originals, which makes for a delightful reading experience; I found myself skipping back and forth between them to better appreciate the authors’ ingenuity.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/27/books/review/27Otherworldly/27Otherworldly-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 657, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/27/books/review/27Otherworldly/27Otherworldly-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 657}}, {'r