e': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/851f1e73-50f7-5d6d-b074-3f18fd7cacef', 'word_count': 866, 'uri': 'nyt://article/851f1e73-50f7-5d6d-b074-3f18fd7cacef'}, {'abstract': 'The pilot is a profoundly destabilizing experience for newcomers and fans of the original alike, delivering on ideas and spectacle in equal measure.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/20/arts/television/watchmen-premiere-recap.html', 'snippet': 'The pilot is a profoundly destabilizing experience for newcomers and fans of the original alike, delivering on ideas and spectacle in equal measure.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the lead-up to “Watchmen,” Damon Lindelof’s “remix,” as he has described it, of the graphic novel by Alan Moore and Dave Gibbons, the question on many viewers’ minds has been, “Should I read the book?” Judging by the explainers and pocket histories in culture pages across the media — including this excellent one by The Times’s Adam Sternbergh — there’s some agreement that the context is helpful, since the events in that book are fully integrated into the TV show.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/20/arts/20watchmen-recap/20watchmen-history10-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/20/arts/20watchmen-recap/20watchmen-history10-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/20/arts/20watchmen-recap/20watchmen-history10-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/20/arts/20watchmen-recap/2