 crawled from the water and began to live on mosses and other land plants. Although drab in appearance, the creature had a glorious future: It would become the ancestor of the 160,000 species of moths and butterflies that populate Earth today.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/21/science/21tb-butterflies-2/21tb-butterflies-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 641, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/21/science/21tb-butterflies-2/21tb-butterflies-2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 641}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/21/science/21tb-butterflies-2/21tb-butterflies-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 959, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/21/science/21tb-butterflies-2/21tb-butterflies-2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1917, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/21/science/21tb-butterflies-2/21tb-butterflies-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/10/21/science/21tb-butterflies-2/21tb-butterflies-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/21/science/21tb-butterflies-2/21tb-butterflies-2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How the Butterfly Discovered Daylight', 'kicker': 'trilobites', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nocturnal No More: How the Butterfly  Discovered Daylight', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Butterflies and Moths', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evolution (Biology)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paleontology', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fossils', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Biology and Biochemistry', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ears and Hearing', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kawahara, Akito Y', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'your-feed-science', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-10-21T19:24:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Wade', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wade', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/43f9ed22-455b-50be-90af-1289e3800f59', 'word_count': 696, 'uri': 'nyt://article/43f9ed22-455b-50be-90af-1289e3800f59'}, {'abstract': 'The big companies have deeper pockets, which helps with both marketing and renovating. That’s one reason some independents are joining the chains as affiliates.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/21/business/independent-hotels-airbnb-boutique-chains.html', 'snippet': 'The big companies have deeper pockets, which helps with both marketing and renovating. That’s one reason some independents are joining the chains as affiliates.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This sh