//www.nytimes.com/2019/10/23/arts/design/colombian-artist-carolina-caycedo.html', 'snippet': 'Carolina Caycedo’s artwork examines the social and environmental impact of harnessing rivers to generate power.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Carolina Caycedo’s images, rivers and streams seem to rear up on hind legs. Waterfalls flow backward and sideways, or fan out into kaleidoscopic formations. Her “water portraits,” as she calls them, come with an aesthetic agenda: to reject the convention of the horizontal landscape format as a way to represent nature, a format that she believes has placed humans in a superior position outside of our natural ecosystems.', 'print_section': 'F', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/27/multimedia/27sp-caycedo1/merlin_161624460_cbae0481-e210-4df6-9f4c-822392e42d3c-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 438, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/27/multimedia/27sp-caycedo1/merlin_161624460_cbae0481-e210-4df6-9f4c-822392e42d3c-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 438}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/27/multimedia/27sp-caycedo1/merlin_161624460_cbae0481-e210-4df6-9f4c-822392e42d3c-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 748, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/27/multimedia/27sp-caycedo1/merlin_161624460_cbae0481-e210-4df6-9f4c-822392e42d3c-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1496, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/27/multimedia/27sp-caycedo1/27sp-caycedo1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75,