'Malia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wollan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4700f069-80a4-53aa-814d-cb9f7c575180', 'word_count': 366, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4700f069-80a4-53aa-814d-cb9f7c575180'}, {'abstract': 'Nancy Princenthal’s new book is about how women in the 1970s took an experience that had been artistically mined by men for millenniums and found ways to convey it in their own terms.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/24/books/review-unspeakable-acts-nancy-princenthal.html', 'snippet': 'Nancy Princenthal’s new book is about how women in the 1970s took an experience that had been artistically mined by men for millenniums and found ways to convey it in their own terms.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “Unspeakable Acts,” a new book about how artists have made sense (or not) of sexual violence against women, Nancy Princenthal draws a subtle but crucial distinction: Just because an act was long deemed “unspeakable” didn’t necessarily mean that it wasn’t shown. In art as in life, abuse has been a constant; what changed in the 1970s, Princenthal says, is who depicted the experience, and how it was consequently understood.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/31/books/30bookprincenthal3/30bookprincenthal3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 449, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/31/books/30bookprincenthal3/30bookprincenthal3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 449}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/31/books/30bookprincenthal3/30bookprincenthal3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 766, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo',