': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd03baa3-d8d0-5466-b088-9cbeb4765122', 'word_count': 709, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd03baa3-d8d0-5466-b088-9cbeb4765122'}, {'abstract': 'The latest crop of horror fiction — some new, some reissued — is truly the stuff of nightmares.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/24/books/review/fall-horror-fiction.html', 'snippet': 'The latest crop of horror fiction — some new, some reissued — is truly the stuff of nightmares.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What to do when you discover that your favorite author (or comedian, or filmmaker, or actor) is a bigot? For some, it inspires a total rejection of the artist, whose work becomes suddenly intolerable. Others reassess their taste, wondering how they were ever able to enjoy a novel with pedophilia at its center, or the stand-up routine filled with misogyny. Still others try to quarantine the art from the artist, as if one might be spared the contagion of the other. And for some — like the horror writer Victor LaValle, who discovered his childhood hero H. P. Lovecraft to be a racist — it’s a long process of soul-searching that ends in compromise.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/27/books/review/27Horror-Trussoni/27Horror-Trussoni-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 425, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/27/books/review/27Horror-Trussoni/27Horror-Trussoni-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 425}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/27/books/review/27Horror-Trussoni/27Horror-Trussoni-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 726, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/27/books/review/27Horror-Trussoni/27Horror-Trussoni-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1451, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/27/books/review/27Horror-Trussoni/27Horror-Trussoni-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/10/27/books/review/27Horror-Trussoni/27Horror-Trussoni-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/27/books/review/27Horror-Trussoni/27Horror-Trussoni-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Something Wicked This Way Comes', 'kicker': 'Roundup', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Horror', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Suicide Woods: Stories (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Monster, She Wrote: The Women Who Pioneered Horror and Speculative Fiction (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'LaValle, Victor D', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Klinger, Leslie S', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lovecraft, H P', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Percy, Benjamin', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'de Moor, Marente', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The New Annotated H.P. Lovecraft (Book)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Dutch Maiden (Book)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-10-24T09:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Danielle Trussoni', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Danielle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Trussoni', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f61fff00-be7b-55ac-a8f8-c129d6a1220e', 'word_count': 2186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f61fff00-be7b-55ac-a8f8-c129d6a1220e'}, {'abstract': 'On deployment in Iraq, we were lucky at first. Two weeks later, we became the first company in our battalion to lose a Marine.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/24/magazine/marine-sniper-iraq.html', 'snippet': 'On deployment in Iraq, we were lucky at first. Two weeks later, we became the first company in our battalion to lose a Marine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We had all been dodging bullets and shrapnel like young matadors, twisting and gyrating our hips, whipping our flak jackets to the side like capes as the superheated shards of metal charged at us. Most of the rounds fired at our forward operating base were so poorly placed that they had to have a dash of luck to even hit a guard post. They were the untrained shots of amateurs, taken by fathers and brothers who were thrust into war by either circumstance or their ideas about the sacred duty of jihad, neither of which involved the training of a professional soldier. It was our brief age of innocence during Second Battalion, Third Marines’ deployment to Iraq in 2006. We had been largely unscathed by the true essence of war. Roadside bombs had left marks on a few of us. Concussions caused by grenades were joked about. Shrapnel left in the fleshy tissue of camouflaged buttocks were a source of hilarity. A Marine named Gabriel Odum had been shot on his first patrol, the bullet passing through his right hand and one lung, but a few lucky centimeters away from fatal. We believed we were invincible. The casualties were serious, but everyone would walk away, bearing the kinds of scars that would make them celebrities at their respective V.F.W. cantinas.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/24/magazine/24atwar-sniper-top/merlin_16021639_37444519-9143-4bfb-a427-08fcf8c669ed-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/24/magazine/24atwar-sniper-top/merlin_16021639_37444519-9143-4bfb-a427-08fcf8c669ed-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/24/magazine/24atwar-sniper-top/merlin_16021639_37444519-9143-4bfb-a427-08fcf8c669ed-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/24/magazine/24atwar-sniper-top/merlin_16021639_37444519-9143-4bfb-a427-08fcf8c669ed-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/24/magazine/24atwar-sniper-top/24atwar-sniper-top-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/10/24/magazine/24atwar-sniper-top/24atwar-sniper-top-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/24/magazine/24atwar-sniper-top/24atwar-sniper-top-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Day a Sniper Shattered My Marine Unit’s Innocence', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Marine Corps', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Haditha (Iraq)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Iraq War (2003-11)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-10-24T09:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kacy Tellessen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kacy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tellessen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba74521e-adec-5dce-aa41-195de90ea8a5', 'word_count': 1392, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba74521e-adec-5dce-aa41-195de90ea8a5'}, {'abstract': '“Stamina is the real challenge: a hoarse voice, a tired brain and a sore ass. But as with all the spoken arts, the only bad experience is bad writing.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/24/books/review/john-lithgow-by-the-book-interview.html', 'snippet': '“Stamina is the real challenge: a hoarse voice, a tired brain and a sore ass. But as with all the spoken arts, the only bad experience is bad writing.”', 'lead_paragraph': '“Stamina is the real challenge: a hoarse voice, a tired brain and a sore ass,” says the actor and author, whose latest book is “Dumpty: The Age of Trump in Verse.” “But as with all the spoken arts, the only bad experience is bad writing.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'cr