': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/27/books/review/27Finder-SUB01/27Finder-SUB01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/10/27/books/review/27Finder-SUB01/27Finder-SUB01-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/27/books/review/27Finder-SUB01/27Finder-SUB01-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Imagining the World of Nazi Hunters', 'kicker': 'fiction', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chasing Evil', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Accomplice (Book)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kanon, Joseph', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-10-25T16:03:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joseph Finder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finder', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/282bf83b-d1f5-5ceb-b003-94cad1f0f661', 'word_count': 666, 'uri': 'nyt://article/282bf83b-d1f5-5ceb-b003-94cad1f0f661'}, {'abstract': 'From seasoned experts to debut authors, these authors write female-focused crime fiction like you’ve never seen before.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/25/books/review/godmother-hannelore-cayre-thrillers-by-women.html', 'snippet': 'From seasoned experts to debut authors, these authors write female-focused crime fiction like you’ve never seen before.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There has never been a more exciting or challenging time for women who write and read thrillers and crime novels. Our bodies and our actions — from the medical to the moral — are on trial every day, which can make it difficult to immerse oneself in books that touch on the further brutality of women. And yet, women crime novelists have utterly captured the genre, pushing its boundaries in important, exciting and creative ways. Writers such as Megan Abbott, Laura Lippman, Alafair Burke and Tana French, to name just a few, give us powerful female characters who upend the stereotypes of their gender. Some of them are cunning, others damaged or deranged. These characters appear on all sides of investigations as victims, investigators and even criminals, reinvigorating the genre as they break down its barriers.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/27/books/review/27Thrillers-Pochoda/27Thrillers-Pochoda-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 425, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/27/books/review/27Thrillers-Pochoda/27Thrillers-Pochoda-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 425}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/27/books/review/27Thrillers-Pochoda/27Thrillers-Pochoda-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 726, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/27/books/review/27Thrillers-Pochoda/27Thrillers-Pochoda-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1451, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url'