onomy. If you pay attention to that indicator only, it would certainly seem like we’re now in the best of times: Gross domestic product has grown for 124 straight months, and we are now officially in the longest economic expansion in recorded American history.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/24/opinion/24Boushey/24Boushey-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/24/opinion/24Boushey/24Boushey-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/24/opinion/24Boushey/24Boushey-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/24/opinion/24Boushey/24Boushey-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/24/opinion/24Boushey/24Boushey-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/10/24/opinion/24Boushey/24Boushey-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/24/opinion/24Boushey/24Boushey-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Way We Measure the Economy Obscures What Is Really Going On', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gross Domestic Product', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economics (Theory and Philosophy)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bureau of Economic Analysis', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Commerce Department', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kuznets, Simon S', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Piketty, Thomas', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Saez, Emmanuel', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zucman, Gabriel', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-10-28T10:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Heather Boushey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Heather', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Boushey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/e059ec64-01cb-540f-a721-5b2d70d83016', 'word_count': 1171, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e059ec64-01cb-540f-a721-5b2d70d83016'}, {'abstract': 'The Tennessee River is one of the most ecologically diverse rivers in the world. It is also one of the most polluted.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/28/opinion/tennessees-dangerous-waters.html', 'snippet': 'The Tennessee River is one of the most ecologically diverse rivers in the world. It is also one of the most polluted.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NASHVILLE — From a distance, a river looks less like a feature of the terrain than like a massive, mysterious animal. A river is a sl