TNow', 'section_name': 'Briefing', 'byline': {'original': 'By Victoria Shannon and Hiroko Masuike', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Victoria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shannon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Hiroko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Masuike', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'briefing', '_id': 'nyt://article/3b110850-e0d5-5c32-9931-af26453649e8', 'word_count': 1082, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3b110850-e0d5-5c32-9931-af26453649e8'}, {'abstract': 'Selective deficit hysteria has done immense damage.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/28/opinion/us-budget-deficit.html', 'snippet': 'Selective deficit hysteria has done immense damage.', 'lead_paragraph': 'So the federal budget deficit just hit $1 trillion (actually $984 billion, but close enough). That’s about $300 billion more than the Congressional Budget Office was projecting in the summer of 2017, before the Trump tax cut was enacted. And basically everybody yawned.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/31/opinion/28Krugman-1/merlin_138761394_c1b643fe-9f76-4dd0-b328-3f73f6a89f9f-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 429, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/31/opinion/28Krugman-1/merlin_138761394_c1b643fe-9f76-4dd0-b328-3f73f6a89f9f-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 429}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/31/opinion/28Krugman-1/merlin_138761394_c1b643fe-9f76-4dd0-b328-3f73f6a89f9f-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 731, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': No