riginal': 'By Javier C. Hernández', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Javier', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Hernández', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6e3bc1f-92cc-5ffc-a191-efad7bcd544e', 'word_count': 884, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6e3bc1f-92cc-5ffc-a191-efad7bcd544e'}, {'abstract': 'The obvious cardiac tests revealed nothing. It wasn’t until one doctor thought creatively that the culprit was found.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/30/magazine/why-did-the-young-mother-have-searing-head-pain-and-a-racing-heart.html', 'snippet': 'The obvious cardiac tests revealed nothing. It wasn’t until one doctor thought creatively that the culprit was found.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Please find something wrong with me,” the 28-year-old woman pleaded. For nearly a year, she’d been looking for a reason for the strange symptoms that now dominated her life. Dr. Raphael Sung, a cardiologist specializing in finding and fixing abnormal heart rhythms at National Jewish Health hospital in Denver, was surprised by her reaction to the news that her heart was normal. Most patients are happy to get that report. For this patient, it seemed like just one more dead end.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/03/magazine/03mag-diagnosis-1/03mag-diagnosis-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/03/magazine/03mag-diagnosis-1/03mag-diagnosis-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 600}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/03/magazine/03mag-diagnosis-1/03mag-diagnosis-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 