'value': 'England', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-10-30T09:00:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lisa W. Foderaro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lisa', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Foderaro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a6931d00-ff7a-528a-bb96-b90a388bbdf0', 'word_count': 2326, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a6931d00-ff7a-528a-bb96-b90a388bbdf0'}, {'abstract': 'The HBO show draws drama and comedy from the plutocrats who order so much of American life. But it’s just as deft at manipulating its viewers’ feelings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/30/magazine/succession-hbo-tom-chicken.html', 'snippet': 'The HBO show draws drama and comedy from the plutocrats who order so much of American life. But it’s just as deft at manipulating its viewers’ feelings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For more than two decades, the professional class has soothed its Sunday-night dread with prestige television’s tales of striving, scheming, anguish, greed and office politics. Underneath all those encomia to the acting and writing on “The Sopranos” or “Mad Men” or “Game of Thrones” was a baser pleasure: the gleeful catharsis of watching someone who isn’t us get crushed under the wheel. What better way to steel oneself for the workweek?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/03/magazine/03mag-screenland-1/03mag-screenland-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 336, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/03/magazine/03mag-screenland-1/03mag-screenland-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 336}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/03/magazine/03mag-screenland-1/03mag-screenland-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 573, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/03/magazine/03mag-screenland-1/03mag-screenland-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1146, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/03/magazine/03mag-screenland-1/03mag-screenland-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2019/11/03/magazine/03mag-screenland-1/03mag-screenland-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/03/magazine/03mag-screenland-1/03mag-screenland-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Succession’ Knows How to Toy with Your Class Rage. Hence the Chicken.', 'kicker': 'Screenland', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Succession (TV Program)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Home Box Office', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2019-10-30T09:00:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michelle Dean', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michelle', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dean', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/18dd31b8-692e-5477-ae33-d8f9d941ff13', 'word_count': 1246, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18dd31b8-692e-5477-ae33-d8f9d941ff13'}, {'abstract': 'If you want to know why his biting “Parasite” is the film of the year, look at the director’s body of work, a deeply humane vision of rotting humanity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/30/movies/bong-joon-ho-parasite.html', 'snippet': 'If you want to know why his biting “Parasite” is the film of the year, look at the director’s body of work, a deeply humane vision of rotting humanity.', 'lead_paragraph': '“It’s so metaphorical!” Kim Ki-woo exclaims early in “Parasite,” Bong Joon Ho’s new film. Ki-woo is the college-aged son of one of the two families — the impoverished Kims and the wealthy Parks — whose fates entwine with horrible and hilarious results. He uses the phrase a few times, most notably with reference to the large, decorative “landscape rock” that is a gift from a better-off friend. In the interpretation of “Parasite” that emphasizes the movie’s fairy-tale aspects, the stone brings good fortune to Ki-woo, his sister and their parents, even as, like so many magical objects, it also curses them. (Spoilers follow, for “Parasite” and other Bong movies.)', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/03/arts/03BongJoonHoCover/03BongJoonHoCover-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 675, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/11/03/arts/03BongJoonHoCover/03BongJoonHoCover-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 675}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/11/03/arts/03BongJoonHoCover/03BongJoonHoCover-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 910, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo'