irstname': 'Jochen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bittner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3fa0685-f5b0-59a7-9cc9-1a81d1036c2a', 'word_count': 894, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3fa0685-f5b0-59a7-9cc9-1a81d1036c2a'}, {'abstract': 'John Carpenter’s formative slasher movie airs on AMC, alongside lots of other creepy Halloween offerings.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2019/10/31/arts/television/whats-on-tv-thursday-the-original-halloween.html', 'snippet': 'John Carpenter’s formative slasher movie airs on AMC, alongside lots of other creepy Halloween offerings.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HALLOWEEN (1978) 7 p.m. on AMC. “This is an evil that makes no facial expression, doesn’t have a voice, moves slowly but kills with a vengeance,” Jamie Lee Curtis said in an interview with The New York Times last year, answering a question about why the creepy killer Michael Myers has remained a compelling figure for four decades. This is the original John Carpenter slasher movie that spawned a franchise. “Halloween” introduced both Curtis (it was her film debut) and her character, Laurie Strode, a teenage babysitter who is stalked by the masked psychopath Myers (Nick Castle). “This is a universal terror that you combine with a woman representing everyone,” Curtis added, “and you have a recipe for people going to the movie over and over again.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2019/10/31/arts/31tvcol-halloween/31tvcol-halloween-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 401, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2019/10/31/arts/31tvcol-halloween/31tvcol-halloween-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 401}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': N